On Wed, 14 Dec 2005, L wrote:
>
> What I'd like to bring to the table now is the age-old topic of
> incongruous gaze attack protection. You see, currently it goes like
> this:
>
> * All gaze attacks can be stopped with blindness, but
> * Only Medusa's gaze and a floating eye's passive attack can be
> reflected by Reflection.
> * Also, only Medusa's gaze, a floating eye's passive attack, and an
> umber hulk's confusing gaze (but _not_ Yeenoghu's confusing gaze) can
> be used against them by applying a mirror.
>
> I think I'll make a patch that puts an end to this silliness. I'll fix
> it such that:
> * All gaze attacks can be stopped by [blindness]
> * No gazes can be reflected by Reflection. (Why does reflective armor,
> or an amulet, prevent Medusa from meeting your gaze?)
> * All gazes can be reflected by applying mirrors. (This is active
> interference on your part.)
>
> That makes sense, right?

   Not to me. Medusa's gaze can definitely be reflected (NHINGreek 
mythology, but I mean, come on...). And pyrolisk attacks definitely
shouldn't be stopped by blindness; that doesn't make any sense.

   So I'd propose a splitting of gaze attacks into "evil eye" and
"gaze-meeting" attacks, as follows:

     "Evil eye" attacks can be reflected by Reflection or by (a)pplying
   a mirror, and defeated by blinding the monster. They are not affected
   by player blindness.
     "Gaze-meeting" attacks can be reflected by (a)pplying a mirror,
   and defeated by blinding (either the player or the monster).

Pyrolisks would have an "evil eye" attack; umber hulks and floating eyes
would have a "gaze-meeting" attack. Medusa would be a special case ---
her attack would be an "gaze-meeting" attack that was also reflected
by Reflection (or maybe only by a polished silver shield?).

   Does this system make Reflection over-powered? Are there any monsters
that wouldn't fit neatly into one or the other category? Am I 
misunderstanding the problem --- how do "reflexive" attacks like the
floating eye's fit into this, or are all gaze attacks reflexive?

-Arthur


